    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" subtype="primary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc" resp="#JP" source="#autopsy">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1"/><persName type="attested"><name>Μο<unclear>σ</unclear><gap reason="lost" quantity="19.5" unit="cm"/></name>
                    <lb n="2"/><name>Εὐπολε<unclear>μ</unclear><gap reason="lost" quantity="11" unit="cm"/></name></persName>
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus" resp="#JP">
                <listApp>
                    <app>
                        <note>Text from autopsy</note>
                    </app>
                    <app loc="1"><note>Orsi: Μο[σ...]; 11 cm are missing from the right side at this point</note></app>
                    <app loc="2"><note>Orsi: Εὐπολέ[μου]; 19.5cm are missing from the right side at this point</note></app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#JP">
                <p>Mos[--] son of Eupolem[--]</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary">
                <p>Traces of the sigma and mu at the breaks are visible. Given the missing space (18 cm up to the sigma trace, c.19.5 cm missing), not more than three letters can be missing from line 1, so Μόσχος (attested at Syracuse and Akragas and Tauromenion) seems very likely, or just  possibly Μοσχίων. In line 2, given the missing space of c.11cm (26.5 preserved), only 2-3 letters can be missing, and so Orsi's suggestion of Εὐπόλεμος (attested at Syracuse and multiple other Sicilian locations in the Hellenistic period) is surely correct.</p>
            </div>
